## Proof

Consider the set $S = \{a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_{mn+1}\}$ equipped with the partial order defined by divisibility:
$$a_i \preceq a_j \iff a_i \mid a_j.$$
Since the $a_i$ are distinct positive integers, divisibility is reflexive, antisymmetric (if $a_i \mid a_j$ and $a_j \mid a_i$ then $a_i = a_j$), and transitive, so this is a valid partial order. Note also that if $a_i \mid a_j$ with $i \neq j$, then $a_j \geq 2a_i > a_i$, so the poset order is consistent with the natural order of the indices.

**Chains and antichains.** A *chain* in this poset is a subset in which every pair is comparable, i.e., a sequence $a_{i_1} \mid a_{i_2} \mid \cdots \mid a_{i_k}$ — each element (except the greatest) divides the next. An *antichain* is a subset in which no two distinct elements are comparable, i.e., no element divides another.

**Applying Dilworth's Theorem.** Dilworth's theorem states that in any finite poset, the minimum number of chains needed to cover the entire poset equals the maximum size of an antichain.

We consider two cases:

**Case 1: The maximum antichain has size $\geq m+1$.**
Then there exist $m+1$ elements of $S$ no one of which divides another. This is precisely the first alternative.

**Case 2: The maximum antichain has size $\leq m$.**
By Dilworth's theorem, $S$ can be covered by at most $m$ chains. Since $|S| = mn + 1$, if every chain contained at most $n$ elements, the total number of elements covered would be at most $m \cdot n = mn < mn + 1$, a contradiction. Therefore, by the pigeonhole principle, at least one chain contains $n + 1$ or more elements. Taking any $n+1$ elements from this chain yields a subset $a_{i_1} \mid a_{i_2} \mid \cdots \mid a_{i_{n+1}}$ in which each element (except the greatest) divides the next. This is precisely the second alternative.

In both cases, one of the two desired alternatives holds, which completes the proof. $\blacksquare$

